EXT. BOMBED-OUT CINEMA - ROOFTOP - NIGHT

Half a marquee hangs by its cables. The facade is gone; the screen stands bare and cracked like a tooth. Smoke drifts across a starless sky. Below, a city of broken lamps.

LIEUTENANT FIONNUALA BRADY-ESCOBAR, 30s, short dark hair, torn sleeve, crouches behind a ventilation stack with a scoped rifle across her knees. The scar on her cheek catches the glow of distant fires. Beside her, SERGEANT IKENNA ABARA, 40s, thick-armed, bleeds slowly from a bandage on his thigh. Further back, behind a toppled projector, huddles a civilian in a grease-stained coat: MR. JAMSHID TAVAKOLI, 60s, a projectionist, spectacles cracked.

She lifts the scope. He presses a hand to the bandage.

IKENNA
Anything?

FIONNUALA
Two on the street. One on the balcony opposite.

IKENNA
Ours?

FIONNUALA
No.

She doesn't move. The balcony light flickers. Mr. Tavakoli shifts and a piece of masonry falls from the projector. She flinches. Ikenna grabs her sleeve.

IKENNA
Easy.

FIONNUALA
He's making noise.

IKENNA
He's old.

FIONNUALA
He's loud.

Tavakoli raises both hands, a gesture of apology. She exhales. Lowers the rifle an inch.

MR. TAVAKOLI
(whispering)
I show films here thirty years. I know every board in this roof.

FIONNUALA
Then tell me which ones creak.

MR. TAVAKOLI
All of them. Except those.

He points to a line of beams near the edge, bare against the sky.

FIONNUALA
Can we reach them?

MR. TAVAKOLI
If you crawl.

She nods at Ikenna. He grimaces, rolls onto his stomach, begins to inch toward the beams. Blood darkens the roof where he passes.

She watches him go. Then, quietly, she pulls the old man close and speaks in his ear.

FIONNUALA
When he reaches the end, you follow. Do not stop.

MR. TAVAKOLI
And you?

FIONNUALA
I stay.

He starts to protest. Her eyes silence him. A distant shell whistles in. They duck. The projectionist clings to the screen's iron frame.

Ikenna reaches the beams. Raises one hand: the signal. Tavakoli crawls after him, gray hair dusted white with plaster.

Fionnuala rises into a crouch. Raises the rifle. The balcony light flares. She takes aim.

Below, in the bare screen's reflection, a thousand flickers of fire wink like frames of a lost film.
